+ * refresh and rows above it the old one - a tear - unless the crossing lies
+ * past the last row, i.e. b >= rows*(1 - copy/scan). Then this refresh shows
+ * the old frame whole and the copy, still faster than the scan, leads the
+ * next refresh from the top.
+ * 0175: the danger is a copy FASTER than estimated (earlier crossing), so the
+ * start row is computed for a copy 1.5x faster than the running average.
+ * 0145 inflated the copy time instead, which started copies ~500 rows early
+ * and put a tear in the lower fifth of the screen at the average copy speed.
+ * A slower copy only moves the crossing down; it must still beat the scan of
+ * the next refresh, checked with a 1.5x slower copy. Never start in the last
+ * lines before the wrap, where fetch-ahead may already read the next frame.
+ * The line counter reads >= the total during vertical blanking; 0145 gave up
+ * there and copied unsynchronised (~16 per 1000 presents once frame rates
+ * rose). Now it waits from the wrap, bounded by two refreshes. */
